---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/data/site';
import { url } from '@/lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Control-room dashboard — ind-ds"
  description="Build high-density control-room dashboards with ind-ds: situational awareness, alarm prioritization, reduced visual noise, dark and high-contrast themes, and keyboard navigation."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Use case"
    title="Control-room dashboard"
    lead="A control room watches an entire operation across several screens for hours at a time. The interface has to support situational awareness — surfacing what has changed and what needs attention while staying calm the rest of the time."
    crumbs={[
      { label: 'Home', href: url('/') },
      { label: 'Use cases', href: url('/use-cases') },
      { label: 'Control-room dashboard' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container stack uc-detail">
      <h2>Typical environment</h2>
      <p>
        A staffed control room with large or multiple monitors, sometimes a video wall, running
        continuously. Ambient light is usually kept low, viewing distances are longer than at a desk,
        and the same layout stays on screen for a full shift. Dark and high-contrast themes are the
        norm rather than the exception.
      </p>

      <h2>User roles</h2>
      <p>
        Control-room operators monitor the whole operation and coordinate responses; a shift lead
        prioritises across areas; specialists are called in for specific events. All of them rely on
        the display to tell them, quickly, what is normal and what is not.
      </p>

      <h2>Interface challenges</h2>
      <p>
        Density is high, so every pixel of visual noise competes with the signal that matters. Alarms
        must be prioritised and de-cluttered so a flood does not hide the critical few. Colour must be
        reserved for meaning, not decoration, and readable at a distance. Consistent layout across
        multiple screens helps operators build a mental map, and keyboard navigation matters when a
        mouse is slow or impractical during an event.
      </p>

      <h2>How ind-ds helps</h2>
      <p>
        The alarm components are built for prioritisation: <code>ind-alarm-panel</code> is
        ISA-18.2-aware (HH/H/L/LL), supports acknowledge and timestamps, and sorts unacknowledged items
        first, while <code>ind-alarm-summary</code> gives a compact count for a wall view. Aggregate
        state comes from <code>ind-equipment-dashboard</code>, <code>ind-asset-overview</code> and
        <code>ind-oee-dashboard</code>, with <code>ind-kpi-card</code>, <code>ind-value</code>,
        <code>ind-gauge</code> and <code>ind-sparkline</code> for dense readouts. The design tokens
        reserve status colour for the operational states running, stopped, fault, warning and
        maintenance, and the dark and high-contrast themes are driven by a single
        <code>data-theme</code> attribute. Components ship with visible focus and semantic markup to
        support keyboard operation.
      </p>

      <h2>Example composition</h2>
      <p>A multi-screen control-room layout might be described as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Wall view: <code>ind-alarm-summary</code>, <code>ind-oee-dashboard</code> and a grid of <code>ind-kpi-card</code> tiles.</li>
        <li>Operator screen: <code>ind-app-header</code>, <code>ind-status-bar</code> and <code>ind-asset-overview</code> across areas.</li>
        <li>Attention rail: <code>ind-alarm-panel</code> with unacknowledged-first ordering and acknowledge controls.</li>
        <li>Detail on demand: <code>ind-value</code>, <code>ind-gauge</code> and <code>ind-sparkline</code> readouts for a selected asset.</li>
        <li>Whole surface rendered with the dark or high-contrast theme via <code>data-theme</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Components are named for illustration; none are rendered live on this page.</p>

      <h2>Integration approach</h2>
      <p>
        Use the Web Components or the typed React or Vue wrappers, sharing one token set across every
        screen so multiple displays read as one system. Live values can be bound with the
        <code>@ind-ds/mqtt</code> helpers or your own data layer. Keep the layout identical across
        seats so an operator moving between stations sees the same map.
      </p>

      <h2>Adoption strategy</h2>
      <p>
        Begin with the alarm and overview surfaces, since those carry the most operational weight, then
        standardise KPI tiles and detail views. Validate colour, contrast and distance legibility with
        operators in the actual room and lighting before rolling the layout across every screen.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        ind-ds does not perform alarm rationalisation or shelving logic; it presents the priorities and
        states your engineering process defines, and it is ISA-18.2-aware rather than certified.
        Choosing the high-contrast theme is one input to accessibility, not a compliance guarantee, and
        real distance legibility depends on your hardware, resolution and room. Multi-screen
        orchestration, window management and video-wall configuration live in your application and
        platform, not in the component library.
      </p>

      <div class="panel uc-cta">
        <a href={url('/components')} class="btn btn--default">Explore components</a>
        <a href={url('/getting-started')} class="btn btn--ghost">Get started</a>
        <a href={SITE.storybook} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--ghost">Open Storybook →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .uc-detail h2 { margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
  .uc-detail p { max-width: 68ch; }
  .uc-detail ul { max-width: 68ch; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: grid; gap: var(--ind-spacing-2); }
  .uc-detail code { font-family: var(--ind-font-family-mono); font-size: 0.9em; }
  .uc-cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--ind-spacing-3); margin-top: var(--ind-spacing-8); padding: var(--ind-spacing-6); }
</style>
